> My name is Rohit Dongre. I am writing this email to introduce myself as a prospective Ph.D. candidate in the Environmental Economics program at University of Delaware (Fall 2026). I hold a Bachelor of Engineering degree in Mechanical Engineering from Nagpur University, India, and master’s degree in economics from Vanderbilt University, United States. My educational and professional experience includes fieldwork with the Indigenous people in Costa Rica, research study in Uganda, and policy work with the Bureau of Economic Development in Chicago.
> 
> My interest in environmental economics primarily emerged from my undergraduate studies in Mechanical Engineering, during which I completed a project titled “The Application of Solar in Food Dryer”, which led to the publication of a research paper in the “International Journal of Innovations in Engineering and Science”. In this project, I took care of sustainability and other economic factors of cost utilization. I applied the concepts of ‘Thermodynamics’, ‘Energy Conversions’ and ‘Heat Transfers’ which startled me with how awful the engineering systems had damaged the environment. This experience left me with the realization that environmental hazards present challenges that are intractable when addressed solely within the confines of engineering. Indeed, it propelled me to redirect my career trajectory from engineering toward the social sciences, such as economics. As I seek to learn how policies can be designed in response to varying environmental conditions, my master’s program helped me understand this. Using impact evaluation methods, specifically RCTs, non-experiment and quasi experimental methods in combination with regression, matching and instrumental variables methods, how I would be able to put forth the policy changes required.
> 
> When I worked as a Field Investigator in Costa Rica where I had the opportunity of a lifetime to work with the Indigenous people. I shared discourse with IDB Economists and the Vice Ministry of Costa Rica and carried out a comparative analysis of the wage gap between indigenous people and their non-indigenous counterparts. Leveraging the econometric techniques like Oaxaca-blinder Decomposition and the anecdotal experiences in the field I shed light on the enormous discrepancies of the wage gap which adversely impacted the indigenous people. Together, these projects improved my understanding of underlying economic theories and taught me skills of handling data, and research methods in economics. I learned about different survey methodologies and improved my skills in STATA and R. Further, it also enabled me to develop skills required to perform collaborative research work.
> 
> The first area of interest which I am interested in exploring is that the India is a densely populated country and due to space constraints, it becomes difficult to plant trees as a measure to reduce pollution in densely populated cities. My aim is to propose an optimal control approach on reduction of pollutants from the atmosphere using the smog tower technology with a view to achieve and maintain the integrated concentration of the pollutants below the air quality standard, particularly, with the basic concept not to cross the human health standard that affects human health.
> 
> Further, I am also interested in exploring the role of social stratification based on caste in India. For instance, scheduled caste and scheduled tribes are the marginalized communities (previously untouchables) in India and were deprived of the equal rights and lack of access to social capital due to the lower social status. I am interested in exploring how lack of access to social capital such as the source of drinking water, more particularly the time to fetch water leads to short-term migration, educational and health outcomes in rural India. This research will provide policy inputs in improving the lives of two hundred and sixty million marginalized people.
> 
> To study such questions, I believe your mentorship would be instrumental in guiding me on how to systematically approach them. Given your plethora of work at the intersection of energy, climate and resource management, I would like to respectfully express my ardent interest in your research. Upon reviewing the breadth of your scholarship, I have come to see your work as an important reference point for my own research interests.
> 
> My reflection on pursuing a PhD is that education is the only way through which I can truly build my life. As a first-generation student I want to make the most of this opportunity. Beyond that, I want to learn and grow my knowledge to see things from different perspectives. With that knowledge, I hope to help people and contribute to the community in meaningful ways. I believe that working under your supervision would greatly enhance my learning and provide a solid foundation for my future academic pursuits. I have attached my CV for your review and would be incredibly grateful if you could have a moment to review it. Considering my candidature and keen interest, would you please let me know if I can be a part of your research group?
